pages={
 'methodology': {'path':'/methodology/','title':'Our Penetration Testing Methodology','seo':'Penetration Testing Methodology | Xarmoured','description':'Understand the Xarmoured assessment workflow: authorization, threat-focused manual testing, validated evidence, reporting and retesting.','summary':'A practical, scoped process that makes findings actionable rather than theatrical.','md':'''## 01 — Scope and authorization\n\nWe begin with the assets, applications, environments, access levels, testing dates and business goals. Rules of engagement explicitly identify what is permitted, prohibited, and who to contact if an issue emerges. No testing begins before written authorization.\n\n## 02 — Map the application\n\nWe identify reachable functionality, roles, objects, sensitive workflows and trust boundaries. For APIs this includes object ownership, function authorization and unusual state transitions. For Android, we include the client, its transport behavior and the backend.\n\n## 03 — Manual-first security testing\n\nGuided by relevant OWASP Web Security Testing Guide, API Security Top 10 and Mobile Application Security Testing Guide concepts, we test the assumptions behind access decisions, user input and critical flows. Automation supplements coverage; it never replaces human validation.\n\n## 04 — Validate findings safely\n\nPotential issues are confirmed within agreed guardrails. We capture reproducible evidence and realistic impact while minimizing unnecessary exposure of customer information. We do not chase destructive proof for its own sake.\n\n## 05 — Report and prioritize\n\nYou receive an executive summary, finding descriptions, preconditions, reproduction guidance, risk reasoning and remediation steps. We make it clear where a risk was validated versus where testing was constrained.\n\n## 06 — Remediate and retest\n\nAfter engineering changes, a separate retest checks whether the same failure path has been closed. Retesting scope, window and commercial terms are agreed in advance.\n\n## Boundaries that matter\n\nA penetration test is a time-bounded evaluation, not a guarantee of security or a compliance certificate. Scope, environment realism, access levels and the test window affect what can be concluded.''','cta':'Request an assessment'},
 'assessment': {'path':'/assessment/','title':'Request a Penetration Test','seo':'Request a Penetration Test Quote | Xarmoured','description':'Request a scoped web, API or Android penetration test. Share the asset type, environment, users, timelines and required deliverables.','summary':'Tell us what needs testing. We will use your requirements to discuss a realistic scope and proposal.','md':'''## What helps us estimate a project\n\n- **Assessment type:** Web application, API, Android app or focused retest.\n- **Assets:** Domains, application areas, endpoint count or build information. Do not send credentials or secrets in this form.\n- **Complexity:** User roles, tenants, authentication flows and integrations.\n- **Environment:** Staging versus production and any testing windows.\n- **Timing:** Target dates and any release dependency.\n- **Goals:** Security assurance, customer due diligence, recent changes or specific concerns.\n\n## What happens next\n\nWe review scope, clarify assumptions and decide whether the engagement is a fit. A quote and proposed rules of engagement follow only once requirements are understood. Form submission alone is not authorization to scan, test or exploit a system.\n\n## What not to send\n\nNever include passwords, API tokens, production customer records or sensitive evidence in an initial enquiry. Use an agreed secure channel later in the process.''','cta':'Request a proposal'},
 'contact': {'path':'/contact/','title':'Contact Xarmoured','seo':'Contact Xarmoured | Talk to a Penetration Tester','description':'Contact Xarmoured about authorized web application, API and Android penetration testing, or remediation validation.','summary':'Have a scope in mind or need to understand what should be tested? Start the conversation here.','md':'''## What we can discuss\n\nWeb application testing, API access control and business logic, Android application security, retesting, reporting and realistic engagement scope.\n\n## Before you send an enquiry\n\nShare a business email and a short description of your objective. Do not paste credentials, internal logs, personal data or production secrets into contact forms.\n\n## Working model\n\nXarmoured operates as a remote-first offensive security practice based in India. Specific availability, delivery dates and commercial terms are established during scoping.\n\nFor assessments, the [assessment request](/assessment/) gives us the most useful starting information.''','cta':'Send an enquiry'},
 'how-we-work': {'path':'/how-we-work/','title':'How an Xarmoured Engagement Works','seo':'Penetration Testing Engagement Process | Xarmoured','description':'From discovery and authorized scope to reproducible findings, remediation discussions and retest verification.','summary':'Straightforward milestones, explicit permissions and deliverables your engineering team can use.','md':'''## 1. Discovery call\n\nWe establish why you are testing, what matters, technical ownership and likely scope. No obligation to buy a broad security package you do not need.\n\n## 2. Written scope and rules of engagement\n\nTargets, source IPs where relevant, test accounts, allowed methods, excluded actions, reporting contacts, dates and emergency stop conditions are agreed.\n\n## 3. Assessment\n\nWe map attack surfaces and evaluate high-risk controls using targeted tooling and manual validation. Material blockers and critical issues are escalated according to the agreed process.\n\n## 4. Findings and walkthrough\n\nA report explains impact and reproduction. We can discuss prioritization and what a correct fix should enforce at the trust boundary.\n\n## 5. Retest\n\nIf part of the agreement, remediated findings are tested again with evidence. Any residual risk or inability to verify is recorded instead of silently marked resolved.\n\n## What determines effort?\n\nNumber of roles and tenants, sensitive workflows, API surface, build stability, test account access, integrations and operational restrictions usually matter more than the number of visible pages.''','cta':'Discuss your scope'},
 'deliverables': {'path':'/deliverables/','title':'What You Receive After a Penetration Test','seo':'Penetration Testing Report Deliverables | Xarmoured','description':'See the evidence, reproduction steps, impact analysis, prioritized remediation and retest records included in an actionable pentest report.','summary':'A report should shorten the distance between discovery, engineering changes and verified resolution.','md':'''## Executive overview\n\nAssessment objectives, scoped assets, testing limitations, major risks and a prioritized remediation picture for decision-makers.\n\n## Technical findings\n\nEach validated issue includes its affected component, preconditions, reproduction steps, severity rationale, technical evidence and potential impact. Where relevant, we map the finding to a CWE and technical testing standard.\n\n## Engineering-oriented fixes\n\nWe describe the control that needs to change—authorization checks, trust boundaries, data validation or infrastructure behavior—rather than simply recommending a generic patch.\n\n## Coverage and constraints\n\nYou see what was in scope, which roles or environments were exercised, what access was unavailable and where conclusions must be treated cautiously.\n\n## Retest addendum\n\nWhen contracted, a follow-up document records evidence of closure, incomplete fixes and remaining weaknesses.\n\n**Note:** We do not issue an invented certification or promise that any application is vulnerability-free.''','cta':'Request report details'},
 'pricing': {'path':'/pricing/','title':'Penetration Testing Pricing & Scoping','seo':'Penetration Testing Pricing and Scope | Xarmoured','description':'Understand what affects the price of a web, API or Android penetration test and what details help get an accurate quote.','summary':'Pricing follows the attack surface and complexity—not a misleading flat fee copied from a rate card.','md':'''## What changes the quote?\n\n- The type and number of applications or APIs.\n- Different user roles, tenants, authentication methods and privileged workflows.\n- Environment readiness, stable test accounts and documentation quality.\n- The depth of validation required and business-critical functionality.\n- Tight deadlines, retest requirements and additional reporting needs.\n\n## Why we do not list a fake starting price\n\nA five-endpoint internal API is a different engagement from a multi-tenant SaaS platform with dozens of roles. A low headline price that ignores scope encourages shallow testing or surprise charges.\n\n## How to get a quote\n\nProvide a concise description through the [assessment form](/assessment/). We can propose a bounded assessment, exclusions and expected deliverables once the application is understood. No guarantee of a quote or fixed turnaround is implied.''','cta':'Get a scope-based quote'},
 'industries': {'path':'/industries/','title':'Security Testing for Digital Products','seo':'Cybersecurity Testing for SaaS, E-commerce & Startups','description':'Focused penetration testing for SaaS applications, ecommerce platforms and early-stage digital products.','summary':'Different products have different failure modes. We tailor testing to the workflows that carry the risk.','md':'''## SaaS and multi-tenant applications\n\nTenants, user roles, invitations, workspaces, exports and internal APIs introduce authorization edges where a small failure can affect another customer. [Explore SaaS testing](/industries/saas/).\n\n## E-commerce and transactional applications\n\nCheckouts, discounts, carts, order access, admin panels and integrations need more than a list of generic injection checks. [Explore e-commerce testing](/industries/ecommerce/).\n\n## Startups and product teams\n\nA focused assessment around a launch or customer security review can prioritize the workflows that matter most. [Explore startup testing](/industries/startups/).\n\nWe do not claim specialist regulatory accreditation or provide legal compliance certification. We assess the technical security within an authorized scope.''','cta':'Discuss your product'},
 'saas-security-testing': {'path':'/industries/saas/','title':'SaaS & Multi-Tenant Security Testing','seo':'SaaS Penetration Testing | Tenant Isolation & API Security','description':'Manual SaaS penetration testing focused on tenant isolation, access controls, invitations, roles, exports and backend APIs.','summary':'Protect the boundaries between users, workspaces and organizations.','md':'''## Where SaaS applications fail\n\nMulti-tenant software depends on every data access enforcing organization context. A UI that hides another customer’s records is not a security control if the API accepts an arbitrary object ID.\n\n## Assessment priorities\n\n- Tenant isolation across read, edit, export and file-download workflows.\n- Organization invitation, onboarding and membership transitions.\n- Role-based functions, admin actions and privilege boundaries.\n- API object and property authorization.\n- Background jobs, webhooks and integration secrets.\n- Billing and subscription state changes where relevant.\n\n## Result\n\nWe provide evidence of validated boundary failures, exploit conditions and remediation guidance suitable for product engineers. Scope, environment, available roles and integrations are agreed before testing.''','cta':'Scope a SaaS assessment'},
 'ecommerce-security-testing': {'path':'/industries/ecommerce/','title':'E-commerce Application Security Testing','seo':'E-commerce Penetration Testing & Checkout Security','description':'Assess ecommerce access controls, order workflows, coupon logic, administrative functionality and payment integration boundaries.','summary':'Test the trust placed in customers, orders, discounts and backend administrative workflows.','md':'''## More than a checkout scan\n\nA store can have clean automated scan results while exposing order details, allowing discount abuse, or permitting an unprivileged user to access administrative workflows.\n\n## What we focus on\n\n- Customer account and order authorization.\n- Cart, coupon, refund and purchase workflow logic.\n- Product and inventory management privileges.\n- File upload and image-processing workflows.\n- Payment provider callback trust, without claiming to audit the provider.\n- Integrations and exposed API data.\n\n## Engagement scope\n\nWe establish boundaries around live orders, payments and customer information. Potentially destructive tests are excluded unless explicitly approved with safeguards.''','cta':'Scope e-commerce testing'},
 'startup-security-testing': {'path':'/industries/startups/','title':'Penetration Testing for Startups','seo':'Startup Penetration Testing for Web Apps & APIs','description':'A focused and clearly scoped security assessment for startups preparing for launch, customer due diligence or major product changes.','summary':'Prioritize exploitable risk in critical workflows without buying an oversized security program.','md':'''## Security reviews with finite budgets\n\nA startup rarely needs every service in the market. It needs clarity on which assets matter, what an attacker could do and how to fix the most meaningful weaknesses.\n\n## Common assessment triggers\n\n- First production release or major architecture change.\n- Enterprise prospect security questionnaires.\n- New authentication, role or invitation models.\n- Partner and payment integrations.\n- Sensitive data storage and export functionality.\n\n## Recommended approach\n\nIdentify the most consequential roles and workflows, scope a realistic test window and get reproducible, prioritized findings. Broader testing can be planned as the product evolves. We do not promise that an assessment alone meets any customer’s compliance requirement.''','cta':'Plan a focused assessment'},
 'resources': {'path':'/resources/','title':'Application Security Guides','seo':'Penetration Testing Guides & Security Resources | Xarmoured','description':'Practical guides to API authorization, pentest preparation, application security reporting and remediation validation.','summary':'Useful security guidance for the engineers and leaders responsible for real applications.','md':'''## Start with the problem you are trying to solve\n\nThese guides explain common testing decisions and failure modes without promising shortcuts or automated guarantees.\n\n- [How API authorization breaks](/resources/api-authorization-security/): object ownership, function access and server-side trust.\n- [Preparing for an application penetration test](/resources/prepare-for-penetration-test/): permissions, roles, environments and test accounts.\n- [Reading a penetration test report](/resources/pentest-report-guide/): severity, evidence, fix quality and limitations.\n- [Retesting security vulnerabilities](/resources/security-retesting-guide/): how to tell whether a fix actually holds.\n- [Android application security basics](/resources/android-security-testing-guide/): client and backend attack surfaces.\n\nResources are educational, not an authorization to test third-party systems.''','cta':'Request an assessment'},
 'api-authorization-security': {'path':'/resources/api-authorization-security/','title':'API Authorization Testing: BOLA, Roles and Tenant Boundaries','seo':'API Authorization Testing Guide: BOLA, IDOR & Tenant Isolation','description':'Learn how API object authorization and function access fail, what server-side checks are essential and how to validate fixes.','summary':'An authenticated API request is not necessarily an authorized one.','md':'''## Authentication answers who. Authorization answers whether.\n\nMany API vulnerabilities appear after a legitimate user signs in. The backend validates the token but fails to check whether that user may act on the specific object, organization or function.\n\n## Three common failure patterns\n\n**Object-level authorization:** A request using a different document or invoice identifier returns another account's data.\n\n**Function-level authorization:** A regular user can reach an admin endpoint even though the UI hides it.\n\n**Property-level authorization:** A client can modify fields that should be server-managed, such as tenant ownership or elevated roles.\n\n## What a good check looks like\n\nFor every sensitive backend action, identify the actor, requested action, target object, tenant and policy. Resolve object ownership on the server, enforce least privilege, and deny unexpected states by default.\n\n## What to verify in a retest\n\nTest both read and write operations, different user roles, cross-tenant requests, exports and indirect access through integrations. A frontend control or an identifier format change is not sufficient remediation.\n\nSee our [API security testing service](/services/api-security-testing/) if you need a scoped independent review.''','cta':'Test API authorization'},
 'prepare-for-penetration-test': {'path':'/resources/prepare-for-penetration-test/','title':'How to Prepare Your Web App or API for a Penetration Test','seo':'How to Prepare for a Penetration Test | Practical Checklist','description':'Prepare endpoints, credentials, user roles, test environments, scope boundaries and incident contacts for a useful penetration test.','summary':'Better preparation means less time lost on access problems and more time testing meaningful risk.','md':'''## Define why the assessment is happening\n\nIs the goal a product release, customer due diligence, risk reduction or verification of specific functionality? This determines priorities and depth.\n\n## Supply representative access\n\nPrepare dedicated test identities for normal users, organization admins, privileged functions and separate tenants if the application is multi-tenant. Avoid sharing personal employee accounts.\n\n## Stabilize the environment\n\nChoose a staging environment that mirrors production security decisions or agree on production-safe constraints. Note external integrations and features that cannot be safely exercised.\n\n## Document the attack surface\n\nProvide base URLs, API specifications or collections, login flows, role descriptions, relevant mobile builds and expected business behavior. Unmaintained documentation should be identified as such.\n\n## Agree on escalation and exclusions\n\nSet authorized targets, testing dates, emergency contacts, prohibited activity and data-handling expectations. Initial enquiry is not authorization to test.\n\n## Plan for remediation\n\nReserve engineering time after delivery. A report without ownership of fixes has limited value. Include an agreed retest window where possible.''','cta':'Prepare your scope'},
 'pentest-report-guide': {'path':'/resources/pentest-report-guide/','title':'How to Evaluate a Penetration Testing Report','seo':'How to Read a Penetration Test Report | Xarmoured','description':'Understand executive summaries, technical reproduction, severity rationale, limitations and remediation quality in a penetration test report.','summary':'A useful report should be convincing to both engineering and management.','md':'''## A good finding is reproducible\n\nLook for affected URLs or components, permissions needed, clear steps, relevant requests or screenshots, impact and recommended changes. A scanner flag without validation is not the same as an exploitability assessment.\n\n## Severity needs context\n\nScores and labels help prioritize, but business exposure, sensitivity, preconditions and attack chaining matter. Prioritization should not rely on a CVSS number alone.\n\n## Understand the limitations\n\nNo test covers everything. The report should identify access constraints, excluded assets, test accounts, time boundaries and areas that could not be verified.\n\n## Recommendations should target the control\n\n“Validate input” or “apply authentication” is rarely enough. For authorization issues, for example, remediation should enforce object and tenant access on the server.\n\n## Closure needs evidence\n\nA developer comment saying “fixed” is not the same as retesting. Ask for verification results and any residual risk.''','cta':'See our deliverables'},
 'security-retesting-guide': {'path':'/resources/security-retesting-guide/','title':'Security Retesting: How to Confirm a Vulnerability Is Fixed','seo':'Security Retesting Guide | Validate Vulnerability Fixes','description':'Learn what penetration test retesting checks, why ticket closure is not evidence, and how to avoid incomplete security fixes.','summary':'The relevant question is whether the original attack path is actually closed.','md':'''## Fixing code is not the finish line\n\nA patch can change one endpoint while leaving a second path vulnerable. An access-control fix can work for reads but fail for exports or background jobs.\n\n## Recreate the original conditions\n\nIdentify the original actor, permissions, object, request and expected impact. Recheck the same trust boundary after the fix in a representative environment.\n\n## Probe nearby variants\n\nWithin the agreed scope, consider alternate verbs, object identifiers, roles, endpoints and state transitions that exercise the same control.\n\n## Record what changed\n\nDistinguish verified resolution, partial remediation, still reproducible and cannot verify due to environment constraints. Preserve evidence without unnecessary sensitive data.\n\n## Prevent regressions\n\nTurn the root cause into automated authorization and security regression tests where appropriate. Independent retesting complements, rather than replaces, engineering tests.''','cta':'Request a retest'},
 'android-security-testing-guide': {'path':'/resources/android-security-testing-guide/','title':'Android Application Security Testing: What Should Be Covered?','seo':'Android Application Security Testing Checklist | Xarmoured','description':'Review Android APK exposure, local data storage, exported components, deep links, network handling and backend API risks.','summary':'The APK, the device and the backend form one connected attack surface.','md':'''## The client is not a trusted boundary\n\nAn Android app runs in an environment controlled by its user. Security-sensitive authorization decisions must be enforced by the backend regardless of what the app UI permits.\n\n## Application package review\n\nCheck embedded secrets, debuggable builds, unsafe permissions, exported components and configuration. Finding a hard-coded key is important only after understanding what access it grants.\n\n## Local state and inter-app exposure\n\nEvaluate sensitive storage, exported activities and providers, deep links and WebViews. Consider what another application or a user with device access could observe or trigger.\n\n## Traffic and backend behavior\n\nStudy transport settings, session management and API authorization. Server-side checks matter even when the app uses certificate pinning or obfuscation.\n\n## Deliverable quality\n\nFindings should distinguish exploitable exposure from routine reverse-engineering observations and explain affected user data and remediation options.''','cta':'Scope Android testing'},
 'faq': {'path':'/faq/','title':'Penetration Testing FAQs','seo':'Penetration Testing FAQs | Xarmoured','description':'Answers about scope, manual testing, API assessments, reports, production safeguards, pricing and retesting.','summary':'Clear answers to the questions clients ask before commissioning security testing.','md':'Browse the answers below. For a scope-specific question, [contact us](/contact/) or [request an assessment](/assessment/).','cta':'Get a scoped answer'},
 'about': {'path':'/about/','title':'About Xarmoured','seo':'About Xarmoured | Manual-First Offensive Security','description':'Xarmoured is an India-based remote-first practice focused on manual web, API and Android penetration testing, clear evidence and remediation.','summary':'A focused security practice that values reproducible evidence over inflated claims.','md':'''## What we do\n\nXarmoured provides scoped web application, API and Android security testing plus retesting and remediation validation. Work begins with authorization and ends with a concrete explanation of validated risks.\n\n## Why this approach\n\nProduct teams need to understand which trust boundary failed, why an attacker could exploit it and how to close it. Automated tools are useful, but they cannot replace deliberate testing of roles, tenants, workflows and business logic.\n\n## What we do not claim\n\nXarmoured is an early-stage practice, not a 24/7 managed security provider, compliance certification body or massive consulting agency. We do not invent client testimonials, certifications or service statistics.\n\n## Location and working model\n\nBased in India, remote-first. Delivery logistics, commercial terms and security requirements are agreed during scoping.''','cta':'Discuss your application'},
 'privacy': {'path':'/privacy/','title':'Privacy Policy','seo':'Privacy Policy | Xarmoured','description':'How Xarmoured processes assessment enquiries and contact information.','summary':'This notice describes the intended handling of information submitted to the site.','md':'''## Information we collect\n\nEnquiry forms may collect your name, work email, company, service of interest, project context, optional timeline and message. The hosting and abuse-prevention providers may process technical request metadata. Please do not submit secrets or regulated personal data.\n\n## Purpose\n\nWe use enquiry data to respond to requests, discuss scopes and maintain business correspondence. We do not sell enquiry information.\n\n## Service providers\n\nThis starter website can use Vercel for hosting, Cloudflare Turnstile for anti-abuse verification and Resend for transmitting enquiries by email. Update this notice if your production implementation or vendors differ.\n\n## Retention and requests\n\nRetention periods and the official privacy contact must be set according to actual business operations and applicable requirements. You may contact Xarmoured to request correction or deletion subject to legitimate obligations.\n\n## Important launch note\n\nThis policy must be reviewed and completed with real provider and contact details before the website is publicly used to collect personal information.''','cta':'Contact us'},
 'terms': {'path':'/terms/','title':'Website Terms','seo':'Website Terms | Xarmoured','description':'Terms covering Xarmoured website information, external links and authorization requirements for security testing.','summary':'Information here is not a test authorization or a substitute for a signed engagement agreement.','md':'''## Informational material\n\nWebsite material describes services and general security concepts; it does not create a consulting contract or guarantee any security outcome.\n\n## No authorization to test\n\nYou must have explicit written authorization and a defined scope before performing any testing of Xarmoured systems or third-party systems. A message, public page or security article does not grant permission.\n\n## Engagement conditions\n\nTesting, pricing, confidentiality, data handling, liability and retesting must be defined in a separate written agreement and rules of engagement.\n\n## External links and changes\n\nXarmoured is not responsible for external sites. These terms may be updated as operations evolve. Review before publishing as final legal terms.''','cta':'Request an assessment'}
}

faq=[
('What is the difference between a vulnerability scan and a penetration test?','A scan flags potential issues using automated checks. A penetration test combines targeted automation with manual work to validate exploitability, access control and business logic in an authorized scope.'),
('Do you need access to our source code?','Not necessarily. Most web and API engagements can be scoped as black-box or authenticated testing. Source-assisted review is a separate scope decision.'),
('Can you test production?','Possibly, if operational risks, exclusions, test windows and emergency contacts are explicitly agreed. Representative staging is often preferable when destructive side effects are a concern.'),
('Can you test an application with multiple organizations or tenants?','Yes, tenant separation, object ownership and role boundaries are important test areas. Provide safe test accounts in separate organizations so cross-tenant paths can be evaluated.'),
('How long does a penetration test take?','Duration depends on attack surface, roles, complexity, access and required depth. We discuss a realistic window after reviewing your scope instead of promising a universal turnaround.'),
('Do you provide a compliance certificate?','No. Xarmoured currently provides technical security assessments, not accreditation, legal certification or an assurance that a system has no vulnerabilities.'),
('Will you share proof of vulnerabilities?','Validated findings include reproduction guidance and relevant evidence within confidentiality and data-minimization constraints.'),
('Do you provide remediation guidance?','Yes. Findings explain the failed security boundary and practical remediation direction so engineers can act on the issue.'),
('Is retesting included in every assessment?','Retesting is available, but the scope, timing and inclusion in the commercial agreement must be confirmed in the proposal.'),
('Can we get a quote before creating test accounts?','Yes. A preliminary scope discussion does not require credentials. Test access is arranged securely only after written terms and rules of engagement.'),
('Will you test Android and iOS?','Current mobile capability is strongest for Android applications and their backend APIs. iOS should not be assumed included without a separately confirmed scope.'),
('Do you perform SOC, MDR or 24/7 incident response?','No. These are not part of the services advertised by Xarmoured at launch.'),
('Do you work remotely with clients outside India?','Xarmoured is remote-first and based in India. Cross-border delivery, scheduling, contracting and any jurisdiction-specific requirements must be reviewed during scoping.'),
('What should we send in an initial enquiry?','Share application type, environment, approximate scope, roles, target timeline and goals. Do not send credentials, sensitive data or access tokens through public forms.'),
('Can you guarantee our application is secure after testing?','No finite assessment can guarantee the absence of vulnerabilities. It provides evidence about agreed assets, methods and conditions during the test window.')
]

